# CODEOWNERS: pg_bloat_cause reviews itself, on every pull request.
#
# This is here for one reason. All four repositories in this family are protected by a ruleset
# that requires one approving review, and they have a single author, so the author can never be
# the reviewer. Without this file, that approval has to be requested by hand on every pull
# request, which is a thing that gets forgotten until the merge is blocked.
#
# Naming a code owner here makes GitHub request that review automatically when a pull request
# touches anything, so the request is not a step anyone has to remember.
#
# It names one person, not a team, because there is one reviewer. That is a fact about the
# project today and not a design choice. When a second reviewer exists, this file is the place
# to record it, and the ruleset's required_approving_review_count is the place to make the
# request match.
#
# what is causing dead rows, and what clears it, so a review here should read with that in mind: the question is whether the change
# tells the truth about the thing it reports, not whether the SQL is tidy.
#
# Ownership is deliberately * everywhere, including the release scripts. The release scripts
# are what a user runs against a live cluster, so they are the highest-risk files in the
# repository and they deserve the review rather than being excluded from it.

